  "nameStory": "This powdery mildew gets its scientific name from 'syringae,' which refers to lilacs (genus Syringa). The species was named after its favorite host plant, as it's most commonly found coating lilac leaves and stems with its characteristic white powder. Think of it as the lilac's unwelcome guest that never leaves!",
  "description": "Lilac powdery mildew is a fungal disease that creates white, powdery patches on lilac plants throughout New England. It appears as if someone dusted the leaves, stems, and flower buds with flour or baby powder. The fungus forms thin, white coating that can cover entire leaf surfaces, and in severe cases, it can cause leaves to yellow, curl, and drop prematurely. While it rarely kills the host plant, it can significantly weaken lilacs and reduce their flowering.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "White powdery coating",
      "description": "Look for chalky white patches that feel dusty when touched, easily rubbing off on your fingers like flour or talcum powder",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Lilac host preference",
      "description": "Almost exclusively found on lilac bushes and trees, coating the upper and lower leaf surfaces as well as young shoots",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaf distortion",
      "description": "Infected leaves may curl upward at the edges and feel slightly thicker or more brittle than healthy foliage",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Pattern of spread",
      "description": "Starts as small circular spots that gradually merge into larger patches, often covering entire leaves by late season",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Seasonal progression",
      "description": "Most noticeable in late summer and early fall when the white coating is thickest and most widespread",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found wherever lilacs grow in New England, from suburban gardens and park plantings to naturalized shrubs along roadsides and old homesteads. Thrives in areas with poor air circulation around lilac bushes, especially in partially shaded locations with moderate humidity. Common in residential landscapes, botanical gardens, and abandoned farmsteads where old lilac plantings persist.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Early infections may begin on new growth but are usually not yet visible to the naked eye. Young lilac leaves appear healthy and green.",
    "summer": "White powdery spots start appearing on leaves, beginning small and gradually expanding. Most noticeable during warm, humid periods in mid to late summer.",
    "fall": "Peak visibility occurs as the fungus reaches maximum development, creating thick white coatings on leaves before they drop. This is the best time for identification.",
    "winter": "Fungus overwinters on fallen leaves and in bud scales, remaining dormant and invisible until the next growing season begins."
  },
  "funFact": "This fungus is one of the few plant pathogens that doesn't need wet conditions to spread - instead, it actually prefers dry weather and can spread its spores on gentle breezes, making it nearly impossible to avoid once it's in your neighborhood!",
  "confusionNotes": "Can be confused with other white substances on plants like scale insects, aphid honeydew with sooty mold, or even bird droppings. The key difference is that powdery mildew wipes off easily as a dry powder, while other white substances are either hard to remove, sticky, or come off in chunks. Memory trick: 'Mildew is mighty fine powder that wipes away easy.'",
  "contextualKnowledge": "While called 'lilac powdery mildew,' this fungus can occasionally infect other plants in the olive family, including ash trees and privet. Many gardeners mistakenly think powdery mildew requires wet conditions, but this species actually thrives in dry weather with high humidity - the opposite of most plant diseases.",
  "compareHints": "Unlike other Erysiphe species that attack different host plants, lilac powdery mildew is quite host-specific and rarely strays from lilacs and close relatives. Grape powdery mildew (E. necator) forms similar white coatings but only on grapevines, while pea powdery mildew (E. pisi) affects legumes and has a slightly grayer appearance. Each species in this genus has evolved to specialize on particular plant families, making host plant identification the best way to distinguish between them.",
